/**
@page exa_coulomb_shear Coulomb-consistent elastic shear cycle

@section coulomb_purpose Purpose and literature connection

Two plane-strain elastic layers are first compressed, then driven tangentially
through sticking, sliding, holding, unloading and reversal. The model uses the
corrected Seitz IWG and verifies that traction can change while an interface
sticks. Its analytic reference is the bulk elastic compliance in series with
an ideal Coulomb slider (a Jenkins element), not an additional penalty spring
at the contact interface.

The setup is a manufactured 2D extension of the stacked-block patch-test idea
in Alexander Seitz, Computational Methods for Thermo-Elasto-Plastic Contact,
TUM doctoral thesis (accepted 2019), Sec. 5.2.3.1, Fig. 5.11:
https://mediatum.ub.tum.de/doc/1454145/1454145.pdf . That published example is
frictionless and thermomechanical; the shear cycle here is an adaptation, not
an exact reproduction. The friction projection follows Seitz, Wall and Popp,
Computational Mechanics 63 (2019), 1091-1110, Eqs. (33)-(35),
https://doi.org/10.1007/s00466-018-1638-x .

For the spring-slider reference, see the Jenkins-element discussion in
Y. Song, C. J. Hartwigsen, D. M. McFarland, A. F. Vakakis and L. A. Bergman,
Simulation of dynamics of beam structures with bolted joints using adjusted
Iwan beam elements, Journal of Sound and Vibration 273 (2004), 249-276,
https://doi.org/10.1016/S0022-460X(03)00499-1 . Author-hosted manuscript:
https://www.imperial.ac.uk/media/imperial-college/research-centres-and-groups/dynamics/YS-JSV04.pdf .
This example uses the single spring-slider limit, not the paper's beam dynamics.

@section coulomb_setup Physical model and boundary conditions

The unit-width domain is [0,1] x [0,1]. A void gap of 0.02 separates equal
layers [0,0.49] and [0.51,1]. Their total reference height is H=0.98. HMR,
GEN and XTK construct separate enriched phase fields; contact is integrated on
both nonconformal orientations. Q1 displacements use the compressible
neo-Hookean Wriggers model with E=1000 and nu=0, hence G=500. The friction
coefficient is mu=0.3. No inertia or independent contact history is used.

The bottom face is fixed. The top face closes the gap and adds compression
c=1e-4, giving prescribed UY=-(0.02+c), then follows the horizontal displacement
cycle below. Vertical-side nominal tractions (0,+/-tau/lambda) are manufactured
from the analytic reference to support uniform shear instead of a finite-width
bending response. They are external lateral loads, not prescribed contact forces:
the interface traction and slip must still be found by the FEM contact solve.
The same boundary loads are used in the negative-control comparison.

The reference deformation gradient in each layer is F=[[1,k],[0,lambda]],
where lambda=1-c/H. For this material P=G*(F-inverse(transpose(F))), so
P_xy=G*k and P_yy=G*(lambda-1/lambda). Thus the nominal pressure and friction
limit, per unit reference width and thickness, are
\f[
 p=G(\lambda^{-1}-\lambda),\qquad L=\mu p,\qquad
 K=G/H,\qquad D_{crit}=L/K.
\f]
Numerically, p=0.1020460230, L=0.03061380691 and Dcrit=6.000306154e-5.
The compliance K is the compliance of the two resolved elastic layers, not the
Seitz tangential stabilization coefficient. Contact uses gamma_n from the
Dirichlet SP (factor 10), tangential increment coefficient 500, theta=-1, and
full Nitsche terms. The prescribed outer faces use SP factor 100.

@section coulomb_reference Analytic reference and physical checks

With accumulated signed slip s and imposed top displacement D, equilibrium
and the uniform-layer compliance give tau=K*(D-s). The independent reduced
Coulomb solution at each load increment is
\f[
 \tau_n=\operatorname{clip}(K(D_n-s_{n-1}),-L,L),\qquad
 s_n=D_n-\tau_n/K.
\f]
This is the reduced physical reference, not the trial-traction formula inside
the Seitz IWG. The IWG uses current constitutive traction plus stabilization
times the slip increment. It does not reconstruct a previous constitutive stress.
The EXA checker stores the following analytic endpoint factors; it does not
rerun a reference finite-element solver or call the input's load callback.

<table>
<tr><th>Stage</th><th>D/Dcrit</th><th>tau/L</th><th>s/Dcrit</th></tr>
<tr><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>0.25</td><td>0.25</td><td>0</td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td>0.5</td><td>0.5</td><td>0</td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0</td></tr>
<tr><td>5</td><td>1.5</td><td>1</td><td>0.5</td></tr>
<tr><td>6-7</td><td>2</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr>
<tr><td>8</td><td>1.5</td><td>0.5</td><td>1</td></tr>
<tr><td>9</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>1</td></tr>
<tr><td>10</td><td>0.5</td><td>-0.5</td><td>1</td></tr>
<tr><td>11</td><td>0</td><td>-1</td><td>1</td></tr>
<tr><td>12</td><td>-0.5</td><td>-1</td><td>0.5</td></tr>
<tr><td>13-14</td><td>-2</td><td>-1</td><td>-1</td></tr>
<tr><td>15</td><td>-1.5</td><td>-0.5</td><td>-1</td></tr>
<tr><td>16</td><td>-1</td><td>0</td><td>-1</td></tr>
<tr><td>17</td><td>-0.5</td><td>0.5</td><td>-1</td></tr>
<tr><td>18</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>-1</td></tr>
<tr><td>19</td><td>0.5</td><td>1</td><td>-0.5</td></tr>
<tr><td>20</td><td>2</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr>
</table>

The checker reads the nominal shear/normal force IQIs on the upper boundary
and displacements at (0.5,0.75) and (0.5,0.25). Linear extrapolation through
the two layers gives signed slip
\f[
 s=1.96(u_x^{top}-u_x^{bottom})-0.96D.
\f]
The analogous vertical extrapolation checks closure of the initial gap.
It also checks the individual reference-node UX values, time, dimensions,
mesh topology, the Coulomb bound and nonnegative shear-times-slip work to
explicit tolerances. Negative shear/slip on reversal uses the opposite sign
from the initial slide and must still dissipate energy.

Finite-width sliding and the enriched discretization depart slightly from the
uniform-layer idealization. The shear-reference tolerance is 2% of L and slip tolerance is 1% of Dcrit;
individual UX tolerances are 0.5% of Dcrit, normal force tolerance is 0.5%, and the
extrapolated gap tolerance is 1e-7. These are physical-error tolerances, not
backend-specific or solver-iteration references. There is no assertion of
monotonic spatial convergence or exact finite-sliding equivalence.

@section coulomb_cases Cases, scope and running

All cases are serial and require ArborX. Driver globals select configuration
before loading the input; standalone input defaults are supplied in the input
source. Each CTest case uses its own output directory.

- Coulomb_Shear-Cycle-1-procs: 8x8 background mesh, 20 accepted load increments.
- Coulomb_Shear-SubdividedLoad-1-procs: the same mesh and load path, with midpoint
  increments inserted (40 accepted increments). This is load-path subdivision,
  not a fixed-frame transient time-discretization study. Each accepted increment
  uses unit bookkeeping time; the quasistatic model contains no physical rate.
- Coulomb_Shear-RefinedMesh-1-procs: 16x16 background mesh, 20 increments.

The initial candidate facets are held fixed. Every IWG evaluation still updates
the actual ray coordinates from current fields. Maximum relative sliding is
approximately 6e-5, much smaller than the distance of the quadrature points
from candidate-facet endpoints on either regression mesh. This deliberately
isolates Coulomb consistency from global search, changing contact coverage and
facet-switching behavior. It is not a qualification of global remapping or
large sliding. Newton hard-break is enabled for excessive residual growth.
Because an iteration limit can still return through the workflow, the driver
also checks its ASCII solver trace: every accepted increment must report
convergence and none may report a hard stop before physical fields are checked.
The actual number of Newton iterations is not a regression reference.

Topology references were obtained from fresh serial runs on 2026-11-26. The
8x8 output has 174 nodes, 144 elements and reference-node IDs 100/22; the 16x16
output has 470 nodes, 416 elements and IDs 294/76. Both meshes retain the same
physical references and tolerances. Set gPrintReferenceValues in the driver to
inspect raw values. Timings and iteration counts are diagnostics only.

@code
cmake --build <build> --target Main_Coulomb_Shear --parallel 4
ctest --test-dir <build> -R '^Coulomb_Shear-' --output-on-failure
gnuplot -c plot_results.gp <case-directory>/Coulomb_Shear_history.csv <output.png>
@endcode

Coulomb_Shear.exo contains the displacement fields and force IQIs at every
accepted increment. Coulomb_Shear_history.csv contains checked stage endpoints.
The optional gnuplot script generates a standalone PNG with force-displacement hysteresis and
signed slip. No plotting package is needed to build or run the MORIS example.

@section coulomb_control Former-law control

A temporary executable outside the repository was linked with the former
previous-constitutive-traction Seitz law, retaining the same zero-radius guard,
mesh, loads, material, penalties, tolerances and solver settings. It completed
the baseline cycle, but failed the analytic force/slip checks. At stage 2 the
reference predicts sticking (s=0, tau=0.00765345); the former law gave slip
8.27571e-6 and shear 0.00405604. Its maximum force/slip errors were approximately
91% of L and 106% of Dcrit. The corrected baseline errors were below the 2% force and
1% slip-reference tolerances. The separate Coulomb-bound and dissipation checks
retain their tighter 0.5% scales. No obsolete IWG or test-only legacy switch is retained in
production code.

@section coulomb_validation Verification record

Serial Armadillo debug runs with GCC 13, OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS=1,
OMP_NUM_THREADS=1 and inherited PETSC_OPTIONS cleared passed all three CTest
cases. Maximum endpoint errors relative to the analytic scales were:

<table>
<tr><th>Case</th><th>Force error / L</th><th>Slip error / Dcrit</th></tr>
<tr><td>Cycle</td><td>1.029%</td><td>0.508%</td></tr>
<tr><td>SubdividedLoad</td><td>1.070%</td><td>0.557%</td></tr>
<tr><td>RefinedMesh</td><td>1.214%</td><td>0.416%</td></tr>
</table>

The original indenter and four Seitz indenter forward/sensitivity CTest cases
also passed with their existing numerical references and tolerances. Focused
contact tests passed 730 assertions in 24 cases on Armadillo, including the
unloaded origin; the corresponding Eigen checks passed 682 assertions in 23
cases plus the origin's 48 assertions. The Eigen verification rebuilt the
updated contact sources and fixtures against the configured Eigen libraries;
the new EXA cases have not been run on Eigen or in parallel.

At the unloaded Coulomb origin (zero gap, pressure and trial shear), Seitz now
uses the stick-side generalized derivative at equality in the residual and
both time-level Jacobians. This avoids zero-norm division. A dedicated unit
regression checks zero residual and finite current/previous tangents for both
elastic models and all three theta variants. This is a corner convention;
central finite differences crossing the nonsmooth origin are not used there.
*/
